   {
    "seq": 3,
    "type": "agent_text",
    "text": "I read the part. Here is what I see, before we set up any simulation.\n\n**What the part is:** A sheet-metal style bracket, about 74 \u00d7 28 \u00d7 30 mm, with a plate thickness of roughly 1.9\u20132 mm. It weighs **0.0392 kg** in Structural Steel (the current default material). It has three main sections:\n\n- **Left flange:** a flat plate, lying flat in the X-Z plane, with the two small holes (radius 2.28 mm) that you bolt through. Its faces sit at about y = -0.6 and y = 1.2.\n- **Diagonal web:** a sloped wall that rises from the left flange to the top of the part, joined to it by bends.\n- **Right flange:** a flat plate with the large hole (radius 5.17 mm, axis along Y), where the load hangs. It sits at about y = 6.3 to 8.3, up near the top of the part.\n\nThere are four sharp inside corners, all along the bend lines between the flanges and the web. These are stress concentrations, so I'll need to fillet them before I can judge any stress limit.\n\n**What I need from you before I set up the simulation:**\n\n1. **Fixed faces:** Should the two small-hole flanges be fully fixed? I'd fix the left flange's faces and the bolt-hole area, which is the usual setup for a bolted bracket.\n2. **Load:** Which face carries the load? I'd apply it to the big hole's inner cylinder. How big is the load (N), and in which direction (global X, Y, or Z)? Is it a downward pull, meaning -Z or -Y in this model's axes? Please confirm the direction, since I can't assume it.\n3. **Material:** Is Structural Steel OK, or do you want a different one?\n4. **Safety factor:** I'll use the default of 1.5 on yield unless you set another value.\n5. **Limits:** What is the allowable peak stress (MPa) or the maximum deflection (mm)? Is there a mass target, or do you simply want the lowest mass that still passes?\n\nOnce you confirm these, I'll run the baseline and check its mesh before we look at any lighter designs."
   },
