    "seq": 11,
    "type": "agent_text",
    "text": "The baseline solved and the mesh check passed. Here is where it stands before I propose the next step.\n\n**Baseline (Structural Steel, 20 N pull in -Y on the big hole, both small-hole flanges and their bolt holes fixed):**\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Mass | 0.0392 kg |\n| Peak von Mises stress | 81.5 MPa (84.9 MPa on the finer mesh) |\n| Max deflection | 1.02 mm |\n| Safety factor (required 1.5, the default you accepted) | 3.07 |\n| Reaction balance error | 1.7e-6 (the support reaction matches the 20 N load) |\n\n**Where the peak sits:** at about (-9.5, 2, -5) mm, which is the sharp inside bend between the left flange and the diagonal web. That is a sharp inside corner, so the peak there is mesh-sensitive. The mesh check moved only 4%, so the baseline is reasonably converged, but I'll fillet that corner before judging any design. I'd start with a 2 mm fillet at all four bends (the same radius for each, which you can change).\n\n**Proposed first look (indicative screen, one feature at a time):**\n\n- Fixed for every design: 2 mm fillets at the four bend corners.\n- Feature 1, web thickness: offset the web face by -0.4 mm to +0.4 mm.\n- Feature 2, right (big-hole) flange thickness: offset by -0.4 mm to +0.4 mm.\n- Feature 3, left (bolted) flange thickness: offset by -0.4 mm to +0.4 mm.\n- Objective: minimise mass. Limit: safety factor \u2265 1.5. I'll check the thin-wall limit (minimum wall 1.33 mm) on each design.\n- Cost: 3 features, so 2 \u00d7 3 + 1 = 7 solves, about 36 s each, so roughly 4\u20135 minutes.\n\nTwo things to confirm: is \u00b10.4 mm on each wall an acceptable range, and do you want the bolted flange thickness included, given that it carries the bolt clamp? If you're happy with that, I'll run the first look."
